# Line endings are a fingerprint input.
#
# `runtimeVersion` uses the "fingerprint" policy, and @expo/fingerprint hashes
# the raw bytes of a number of tracked text files (.gitignore, eas.json,
# plugins/*.js, patches/*.patch, config-plugin inputs such as
# lib/config/apple-pay.json). A CRLF checkout therefore produces a completely
# different runtimeVersion than an LF checkout of the same commit — see
# docs/deployment/EAS_UPDATE_POLICY.md.
#
# That is not hypothetical: iOS build 2.0.5(85) (Windows, core.autocrlf=true)
# and 2.0.5(86) (Linux) were the same commit and got different fingerprints,
# purely because of CRLF vs LF. EAS Build and the GitHub Actions OTA workflow
# both run on Linux, so LF is the only checkout that can ever agree with them.
#
# The repository index has always been LF, so this file changes nothing that is
# committed — it only stops Git from rewriting to CRLF on checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files genuinely require CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Never touch binary assets.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.jar binary
*.keystore binary
*.p8 binary
*.p12 binary
*.mobileprovision binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.mp4 binary
*.mov binary
*.lottie binary
